{"case_id":"PMC12215201_01","case_text":"A 56-year-old Puerto Rican male with no past medical history originally presented to the emergency department with complaints of 2 months of fatigue, swollen abdomen, decreased appetite, and weight loss. He also endorsed pain in his abdomen, which prompted him to seek medical attention. The patient never sought any medical care before this and had no prior similar episodes. His social history was negative for alcohol consumption, smoking tobacco, or use of any intravenous recreational drugs. He used to work as a landscaper at a construction company. His family history was noncontributory.\nOn initial assessment, the patient was afebrile, hemodynamically stable, and in no acute distress. Physical examination revealed a diffusely tender and distended abdomen with shifting dullness indicative of ascites. Laboratory results showed hemoglobin 6.1 g/dL, platelet count 63 x 103/mm3, total bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase 315 IU/L, AST 305 IU/L, ALT 209 IU/L, and albumin 2.6 g/dL. An abdominal ultrasound performed showed multiple solid masses in the liver, the most extensive one measuring 2.5 x 2.7 x 2.1 cm. The spleen was enlarged and infiltrated with various masses, with very little healthy tissue and at least one discrete mass measuring 4.1 x 3.8 x 4.6 cm. He also underwent CT of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast, which exhibited multiple hypodense lesions in the liver and spleen with the peripheral enhancement of the contrast (Fig. 1) suggestive of metastatic lesions. There was also evidence of involvement of the lung, bilateral kidney, and bones with massive abdominopelvic ascites extending into the presacral region and bilateral pleural effusions.\nThe patient received packed red blood cells and platelets for worsening anemia and thrombocytopenia, respectively, during his hospital stay. The patient underwent paracentesis twice with removal of 5.2 L and 6.1 L bloody ascitic fluid. The ascitic fluid protein was 1 g/dL and 2 g/dL with serum ascitic albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1 g/dL and 1.1 g/dL, respectively, indicating exudative ascites. The fluid analysis showed a PMN count >250 with cytology negative for malignant cells. To find the possible primary, a colonoscopy was performed, which was normal. Esophagogastroduodenoscopy demonstrated multiple erythematous nodular lesions in the gastric body (Fig. 2) and duodenum (Fig. 3). However, biopsies from the lesions were inconclusive. Video capsule endoscopy showed numerous metastatic lesions seen throughout the stomach and small bowel. The patient received more platelet transfusions for worsening thrombocytopenia. The patient underwent liver biopsy, which showed small aggregates of anastomosing vascular channels with highly atypical cells suggestive of angiosarcoma (Fig. 4). Tumor cells were positive for CD-34 (Fig. 5) and focally positive for factor VIII (Fig. 6). The patient underwent Pleurx catheter placement for palliative support. He continued to have worsening sanguineous drainage from the catheter. Unfortunately, the patient developed disseminated intravascular coagulation as a complication and suffered a cardiac arrest 2 weeks later following which he could not be revived.","graphs":{"projeto2":{"nodes":[{"node_id":"P1","type":"Patient","label":"case PMC12215201_01","attributes":{}},{"node_id":"E1","type":"Exam","label":"hemoglobin","attributes":{"polarity":"present","mentions":"m1","mention_texts":"[\"hemoglobin\"]","spans":"[[838,848]]","char_start":838,"char_end":848,"evidence_text":"hemoglobin","mesh":"D006454","confianca":1.0}},{"node_id":"R1","type":"ExamResult","label":"6.1 g/dl","attributes":{"polarity":"present","mentions":"m2","mention_texts":"[\"6.1 g/dL\"]","spans":"[[849,857]]","char_start":849,"char_end":857,"evidence_text":"6.1 g/dL","confianca":0.6538}},{"node_id":"E2","type":"Exam","label":"platelet count 63 x","attributes":{"polarity":"present","mentions":"m3","mention_texts":"[\"platelet count 6